            <ArticleTitle>Thunderclap headache caused by Erve virus?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>509-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Systematic studies of a possible human neuropathogenicity of the Erve virus have not yet been carried out. In a randomized, blind study 166 patients with viral encephalitis, 46 patients with cerebral hemorrhage, 72 patients with &quot;thunderclap&quot; headache, and 205 healthy blood donors were examined by indirect immunofluorescence for Erve virus antibodies. None of the patients with encephalitis, two patients with cerebral hemorrhage (4.3%), 10 patients with thunderclap headache (13.9%; p &lt; 0.0001), and two blood donors (1.0%) exhibited antibodies against the Erve virus. These results suggest a human pathogenicity of the Erve virus for the first time.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
